- time: 00:01:03
  seconds: 63
  topic: Vote on Agenda Amendment
  desc: The motion to amend the agenda to add public comment was put to a vote and
    passed unanimously by the board members present.
- time: 00:01:12
  seconds: 72
  topic: Chair's Opening Remarks and Apologies
  desc: Chair DeAngelis made a public apology to Members Dowling and Holman for her
    conduct at the previous meeting, acknowledging she was not in her best form. She
    also thanked student members Kabisa and Davison for their commitment to the board.
- time: 00:02:07
  seconds: 127
  topic: Upcoming Dates and Superintendent Search
  desc: 'The Chair announced two important upcoming dates: a board workshop on December
    22nd with Zeal, the organization selected for the superintendent search, and a
    community forum on January 22nd to gather public input, with details to follow.'
- time: 00:03:44
  seconds: 224
  topic: Public Meeting Guidelines and Budget Discussion Context
  desc: The Chair reiterated that the meeting is public and all documents are accessible.
    She explained that the goal for the evening was to provide guidance to the superintendent
    and executive team on a budget range, with an opportunity for public comment later.
- time: 00:05:38
  seconds: 338
  topic: FY27 Budget Development Page
  desc: Assistant Superintendent Johanna Prince introduced herself and directed attendees
    to the FY27 budget development page on the school district's website, where all
    materials would be posted throughout the budget season.
- time: 00:06:10
  seconds: 370
  topic: Budget Presentation Goals and Process
  desc: 'Dr. Prince outlined the goals for the evening: to understand district demographics
    and financial context, provide guidance for the first budget draft, and explore
    long-term financial sustainable models. She explained the process of assembling
    the superintendent''s budget, which then becomes the board''s budget and is passed
    to the city council.'
- time: 00:08:13
  seconds: 493
  topic: Budget Guidelines and Board Norms
  desc: Dr. Prince reminded the board of guidelines for public business, including
    not naming private citizens when discussing positions. She also referenced the
    board's working norms established earlier in the year to guide interactions during
    the budget season.
- time: 00:09:34
  seconds: 574
  topic: Question Protocol
  desc: Chair DeAngelis requested that questions be held until the end of the presentation,
    allowing Dr. Prince to complete her remarks without interruption.
- time: 00:10:39
  seconds: 639
  topic: Superintendent's Budget Goals
  desc: 'Dr. Prince detailed the four main goals guiding the superintendent''s budget:
    attendance, belonging, student achievement, and a commitment to equity, focusing
    on progress for historically marginalized subgroups.'
- time: 00:11:40
  seconds: 700
  topic: Administrative Team Commitments
  desc: The administrative team's commitments for the budget process were presented,
    including using feedback from the previous year, embracing the community school
    strategy, prioritizing students furthest from opportunity, valuing all staff,
    and ensuring fiscal responsibility to taxpayers.
- time: 00:12:48
  seconds: 768
  topic: District Enrollment Trends
  desc: Dr. Prince presented 10 years of enrollment data, showing a decline prior
    to the pandemic, a significant influx of asylum-seeking families post-pandemic,
    and a subsequent decline to the lowest enrollment in a decade, impacting financial
    sustainability.
- time: 00:14:13
  seconds: 853
  topic: Changing Student Demographics
  desc: Data illustrated changes in the student body, including a high water mark
    for multilingual learners, a 5% increase in special education population over
    10 years, and fluctuations in McKinney-Vento eligible students, all of which have
    staffing implications.
- time: 00:16:33
  seconds: 993
  topic: Staffing Composition and Ratio
  desc: The presentation detailed the district's staffing composition across various
    associations (SPTA, SPESPA, administrators, service staff) and non-bargaining
    roles. It also showed the ratio of benefits-eligible staff to students, noting
    that a smaller workforce is anticipated next year due to budget constraints.
- time: 00:18:40
  seconds: 1120
  topic: Property Tax Rate Breakdown
  desc: 'Dr. Prince explained the three components of a property tax bill: school
    district budget, county, and city. She highlighted that the school budget is the
    largest portion and that increases in city or county services could further impact
    taxpayers.'
- time: 00:20:01
  seconds: 1201
  topic: Board Discussion on Property Tax Breakdown
  desc: Board members asked clarifying questions about the property tax breakdown.
    Member Feller requested future data on school performance and spending per pupil,
    while Member Holman sought clarification on the impact of city/county increases.
- time: 00:22:00
  seconds: 1320
  topic: Board Discussion on Budget History and Finance Director
  desc: Member Richardson inquired about the district's history of not having a permanent
    finance director during years of rising budgets. Dr. Prince estimated it was three
    out of five years and confirmed a finance director is now in place.
- time: 00:23:15
  seconds: 1395
  topic: Budget History and Fund Balance
  desc: Dr. Prince presented the history of the operating budget and the amount raised
    by taxes, noting that the school board has managed increases through the use of
    fund balance, which is now depleted.
- time: 00:25:00
  seconds: 1500
  topic: FY27 Budget Forecast Options
  desc: Five options for the FY27 budget were presented, showing varying tax increases
    based on different spending scenarios. Dr. Prince explained that even with flat
    spending, a 3% tax increase is projected due to less revenue and no fund balance
    to draw from.
- time: 00:29:36
  seconds: 1776
  topic: Per Pupil Cost Comparison
  desc: Dr. Prince shared data comparing South Portland's per pupil cost to other
    districts, noting South Portland is at the higher end. Board members requested
    additional comparative data for similar cities like Lewiston, Waterville, Sanford,
    and Biddeford, including special education and economically disadvantaged student
    percentages.
- time: 00:32:50
  seconds: 1970
  topic: Board Discussion on Per Pupil Cost and Structural Issues
  desc: Member Feller questioned why South Portland's per pupil cost is higher and
    if there are structural reasons. Member Rich noted the structural nature of the
    problem with lower enrollments being ignored.
- time: 00:33:04
  seconds: 1984
  topic: Budget Timeline and Next Steps
  desc: Dr. Prince outlined the budget timeline, including stakeholder input, superintendent's
    budget development in January, public review in February, presentations to city
    council in April, and a citizen vote in June. She emphasized that this December
    meeting format is new and designed to gather early guidance.
- time: 00:34:35
  seconds: 2075
  topic: Big Ideas for Financial Sustainability
  desc: 'Four big ideas with potential financial benefits were proposed: redesigning
    elementary school grade level configurations, a single start time for middle school,
    expanded high school/adult education programming, and exploring service unit efficiencies.
    These ideas are estimated to yield significant savings.'
- time: 00:37:53
  seconds: 2273
  topic: Guidance Request to the Board
  desc: Dr. Prince reiterated the administration's request for board guidance on a
    target tax increase for the first budget draft and which of the 'big ideas' to
    explore further for modeling.
- time: 00:39:43
  seconds: 2383
  topic: Board Discussion on Revenue Decrease and Fund Balance
  desc: Member Smith asked about the reason for projected revenue decreases. Dr. Prince
    explained it's primarily due to declining enrollment and the depletion of the
    fund balance, which previously offset tax burdens.
- time: 00:44:34
  seconds: 2674
  topic: Board Discussion on Food Nutrition Budget
  desc: Member Richardson suggested adding the food nutrition budget to the exploration,
    inquiring about state dollars per child for nutrition and how the district's spending
    compares to that amount.
- time: 00:45:51
  seconds: 2751
  topic: Board Discussion on Adult Education Funding
  desc: Member Rich asked for clarification on a significant number related to adult
    education. Dr. Prince explained it reflects increased investment in adult education
    after grant funding expired, serving more adults, particularly multilingual learners.
- time: 00:46:55
  seconds: 2815
  topic: Board Discussion on Early Learning Campus Location
  desc: Member Davison inquired about potential locations for an early learning campus,
    suggesting Kaler Elementary due to its central location. Dr. Prince stated locations
    haven't been determined but would be explored if the idea moves forward.
- time: 00:48:11
  seconds: 2891
  topic: Board Discussion on Early Learning Campus Cost Savings
  desc: Member Davison asked if an early learning campus would be a cost savings or
    an additional expense. Dr. Prince confirmed it's believed to be a cost savings
    by optimizing staff numbers through different student configurations.
- time: 00:49:14
  seconds: 2954
  topic: Board Discussion on Budget Options and Taxpayer Impact
  desc: Member Feller expressed concern for taxpayers on fixed incomes and suggested
    Option B as realistic, but also wanted to see an Option A budget to prompt advocacy.
    He proposed additional savings ideas like solar on schools, bus route optimization,
    hiring freezes, and PTA contributions.
- time: 00:51:06
  seconds: 3066
  topic: Board Discussion on Enrollment Trends
  desc: Member Rich asked for more context on demographic changes and the general
    trend of declining enrollment. Dr. Prince confirmed it aligns with national data
    on declining school-age populations.
- time: 00:51:57
  seconds: 3117
  topic: Board Discussion on Brick Hill TIF and Portland Bus Maintenance
  desc: Member Dowling asked about the Brick Hill TIF district's contribution to education
    and its expiration, and also inquired about the status of renegotiating rates
    for Portland's school bus maintenance. Dr. Prince confirmed the TIF revenue is
    increasing and that discussions are ongoing to rightsize the bus maintenance agreement.
- time: 00:53:41
  seconds: 3221
  topic: Board Discussion on Budget Option Clarity
  desc: Member Dowling stated she wasn't ready to commit to a specific number but
    felt options C, D, and E were incompatible with community capacity. She leaned
    towards a range between A and B, seeking more detailed examples of what each option
    would entail.
- time: 00:55:18
  seconds: 3318
  topic: Board Discussion on Elementary School Redesign
  desc: Member Smith highlighted elementary school redesign as an obvious area for
    savings and requested data on enrollment trends per school to inform future discussions.
    Member Richardson expressed concerns about revisiting this topic, citing past
    community feedback and unfulfilled board recommendations regarding equity consultants.
- time: 01:03:04
  seconds: 3784
  topic: Board Discussion on Affordability and Transportation
  desc: Member Davison linked school segregation to a lack of affordable housing across
    the city and questioned how transportation would work if student populations were
    mixed. Dr. Prince acknowledged the need for creative solutions in bus route redesign.
- time: 01:09:34
  seconds: 4174
  topic: Board Discussion on Property Taxes and Airbnb Impact
  desc: Member Holman commented on the impact of high property taxes due to revaluation
    and the proliferation of Airbnbs, suggesting that converting Airbnbs back to housing
    could alleviate affordability issues and indirectly help the school budget.
- time: 01:10:24
  seconds: 4224
  topic: Board Discussion on Budget Realities and Structural Change
  desc: Member Smith emphasized the financial realities of declining enrollment and
    the need for structural change. He suggested that if elementary school reconfiguration
    isn't pursued, a higher tax increase (closer to Option C) might be necessary to
    avoid significant damage to student instruction and staff capacity.
- time: 01:14:08
  seconds: 4448
  topic: Student Perspective on Cost Savings
  desc: Member Davison, a student, shared her perspective that true education comes
    from people and experience, not just money. She suggested that a lower budget
    option wouldn't significantly impact the district, citing wasteful spending and
    small class sizes as areas for efficiency.
- time: 01:17:40
  seconds: 4660
  topic: Chair's Perspective on Budget and Community Impact
  desc: Chair DeAngelis agreed that four months is enough time for change and expressed
    personal challenges with past equity recommendations. She supported consolidating
    pre-K and K for cost savings and exploring Metro bus use for high schoolers. She
    stressed the need for fiscal responsibility, given the high percentage of the
    budget allocated to education and the impact of tax increases on residents and
    school employees.
- time: 01:24:52
  seconds: 4972
  topic: Dollar Amount of Budget Cuts
  desc: 'Dr. Prince provided the dollar amounts corresponding to the budget options:
    moving to Option C would require cutting $2.3 million, Option B $4.1 million,
    and Option A $7.8 million, offering a different perspective on the scale of potential
    changes.'
- time: 01:25:54
  seconds: 5154
  topic: Board Discussion on Bus Monitors
  desc: Member Holman suggested that if significant savings were realized from a single
    middle school start time, those funds could potentially be used to hire bus monitors,
    referencing recent tragic accidents involving children.
- time: 01:31:01
  seconds: 5461
  topic: Member Richardson's Emotional Plea for Students
  desc: Member Richardson, as the only parent on the board with children in the district,
    passionately argued against Option A, stating it would significantly impact children's
    learning experiences and make teachers' lives more difficult. She criticized past
    decisions to dip into fund balance without considering long-term consequences
    for students.
- time: 01:33:09
  seconds: 5589
  topic: Student Representative's View on Wasteful Spending
  desc: Student Representative Davison reiterated her belief that a lower budget option
    would not significantly harm the district, citing personal experience with small,
    unfilled class sizes and suggesting that wasteful spending could be addressed
    by filling classrooms or pooling resources.
- time: 01:34:26
  seconds: 5666
  topic: Chair's Call for Guidance and Public Comment
  desc: Chair DeAngelis noted the time and sought to finalize the board's guidance
    on the budget range before moving to public comment. She emphasized that the board's
    role was to provide guidance, not to debate amongst themselves.
- time: 01:35:23
  seconds: 5723
  topic: Binding Nature of Guidance
  desc: Member Smith asked about the binding nature of the guidance provided. Chair
    DeAngelis clarified that it's a starting point for the superintendent's proposal,
    which the board can later adjust, and that the city council also provides further
    guidance before a citizen vote.
- time: 01:38:25
  seconds: 5905
  topic: Final Board Guidance on Budget Range
  desc: 'Chair DeAngelis summarized the board''s guidance for the superintendent:
    to prepare a budget with a tax increase target between Option A (3.1%) and Option
    B (9.7%), specifically around 6.5%. She also encouraged listening groups for community
    input.'
- time: 01:39:31
  seconds: 5971
  topic: Public Comment Period Opens
  desc: 'The Chair opened the floor for public comment, explaining the rules: speakers
    should state their name and city, have three minutes, and the board is there to
    listen, not debate. She encouraged speakers to come forward promptly.'
- time: 01:41:09
  seconds: 6069
  topic: Public Comment (Sarah Gagne)
  desc: Sarah Gagne, President of the South Portland Teachers Association (SPTA),
    requested additional data on instructional staff-to-student ratios and administrator-to-student
    ratios. She expressed that Option A felt impossible and urged the board not to
    force staff to defend their positions or programs against each other, offering
    to collaborate on providing information about school programming and opportunities.
- time: 01:44:22
  seconds: 6262
  topic: Public Comment Period Closes
  desc: After no other members of the public came forward, the Chair closed the public
    comment period.
- time: 01:44:51
  seconds: 6291
  topic: Confirmation of Budget Guidance
  desc: The superintendent confirmed the board's guidance to aim for a 6.5% tax increase,
    which is the midpoint between Option A and B. The board members present did not
    offer further comments on this target.
- time: 01:45:50
  seconds: 6350
  topic: Motion to Adjourn
  desc: Member Dowling moved to adjourn the meeting, seconded by Member Holman. The
    motion passed unanimously.
